RISC3D test kit 9 (prototype, 12 November 2026)
===============================================

RISC3D is a 3D driver interface for RISC OS. On RISC OS on Linux its
driver (Linux3D) has Linux's graphics driver do the drawing. This kit is
for trying it on a Raspberry Pi 4 running RISC OS on Linux. Kit 1 drew
GLES 2 on the Pi 4's GPU; kit 2 added OpenGL 1.x and 2.1, what most RISC
OS ports use (GL), and drew it exactly as Linux does; kit 3 made the
calls cheaper on the RISC OS side; kit 4 said where a frame's time goes
(GL's bench lines: the renderer's own time, empty frames, the calls
alone): on the Linux side; kit 5 made that side cheaper; kit 6 sent each
glBegin ... glEnd as one draw, as Mesa does itself; kit 7 sent a long
one in pieces, so Linux draws while the program is still calling, and
GL's bench measures the processor too (RISC OS against Linux); kit 8
wrote the vertices straight into the shared memory (no copy); kit 9
overlaps frames: Linux finishes one while the program draws the next
(Cube uses it; GL's bench runs both ways). The SWI numbers are provisional.

Needs
-----
 * RISC OS on Linux with RiSH (Apps), and the network up.
 * For the GPU: riscos-kernel-pi 6.6.78-9 or later from Updates (it adds
   Linux's driver for the Pi 4 and 5's GPU, v3d), and a restart. With an
   earlier kernel everything still runs, drawn by Linux in software.
 * A 16 million colour screen mode (the cube is plotted as a 32 bits a
   pixel sprite).
 * About 150 MB free on the Linux side for Debian's Mesa.

Steps
-----
 1. Copy the RISC3DTest folder to the top of HardDisc4.
 2. Run RiSH (in Apps), choose "Root shell" from its icon bar menu, give
    the root password, and type:
        sh /riscos/HardDisc4/RISC3DTest/install
    The last lines say what will draw: "GPU" and V3D for the Pi's GPU,
    or "software" and llvmpipe.
 3. Double-click Cube: a spinning cube in a window. Its title bar says
    what is drawing and the frames a second. Run it twice for two at once.
 4. Double-click Test: it draws 300 frames two ways, checks the pixels
    and opens Results.
 5. Double-click GL: OpenGL 1.x and 2.1 (immediate mode, lighting, a
    texture, display lists, vertex arrays, selection) through RISC3D. It
    takes a few seconds and opens GLResults. Step 2 drew the same with
    Linux's Mesa directly, into LinuxResults: the two should match, apart
    from the first line and the list names.

After a restart, do step 2 again (the helper isn't started
automatically yet).

To report
---------
Results, GLResults, LinuxResults, GLPicture and LinuxPicture, the
title bar of the cube window, and the output of step 2. If something fails: helper-log in this folder, and the LOGS
folder as usual.

Known limits
------------
 * Each frame is copied back from Linux and plotted as a sprite: the
   frame rate shows that copy as much as the GPU.
 * Programs don't use it through Mesa's EGL yet: only these tests.
 * The window can't be resized.

To remove
---------
In a root shell: kill $(pidof rol-3d); rm /opt/riscos/rol-3d
*RMKill Linux3D and *RMKill RISC3D. Debian's Mesa can stay.

